# Component Architecture Enforcement

When creating or modifying a component (for example `super-component.tsx`), follow these rules:

- Add or update a component test file to cover changed behavior.
- Keep component `.tsx` focused on rendering and event wiring.
- Move stateful/data logic (state transitions, derived state, query/mutation orchestration, hook composition) into dedicated hooks; create a new hook file when needed.
- Move pure logic (parsing, mapping, formatting, calculations, utility functions) out of the component into:
  - a shared `utils` file when broadly reusable, or
  - a colocated `.helper` file when tightly related to that component flow.
- Extract styles to a colocated `.style.ts` when style blocks are non-trivial or reused.
- Extract component-specific types/interfaces to a colocated `.types.ts` when it improves readability or reuse.
- Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) for component module items when it clearly improves import ergonomics and does not add ambiguity.
- Update imports/usages after extraction and keep naming explicit and domain-based.

## Quick Example

- `super-component.tsx` handles UI only.
- `use-super-component.ts` handles state and query behavior.
- `super-component.helper.ts` handles local pure transforms.
- `super-component.style.ts` holds extracted styles.
- `super-component.types.ts` holds component-specific types.
- `super-component.test.tsx` verifies user-visible behavior.
- `index.ts` re-exports component module items when useful.

## File Layout Example

Before:

- `super-component.tsx`

After:

- `super-component.tsx`
- `use-super-component.ts`
- `super-component.helper.ts`
- `super-component.style.ts`
- `super-component.types.ts`
- `super-component.test.tsx`
- `index.ts`
